import Code from "~/components/Code.astro";
---

<h1>Records &amp; Result</h1>

<p>
  Kex models data directly. A <code>record</code> is a product type. Named, typed
  fields, built with braces:
</p>

<Code
  code={`record Point do
  x : Float
  y : Float
end

let p = Point { x: 1.0, y: 2.0 }`}
  repl
/>

<p>
  A <code>type</code> union describes a value that's one of several shapes. Match
  on it exactly like you matched <code>Just</code>/<code>None</code> in the last lesson:
</p>

<Code
  code={`type Shape = Circle(Float) | Square(Float)

let area(s) = match s do
  Circle(r) -> Math.PI * r * r
  Square(s) -> s * s
end

let c = Circle(2.0)
let s = Square(3.0)

area(c)
area(s)
`}
  repl
/>

<p>
  <strong>Result</strong> is a union built into the prelude (the standard library
  of Kex) for functions that can fail: <code>Ok(value)</code> or <code
    >Error(reason)</code
  >. Write it as either <code>Result&lt;T, E&gt;</code> or you can use the syntax
  sugar <code>T or! E</code> in a signature.
</p>

<Code
  code={`let safeDiv(a, b) -> Int or! String do
  return Error("div by zero") if b == 0
  return Ok(a / b)
end

safeDiv(10, 2)`}
  repl
/>

<Code code={`safeDiv(10, 0)`} repl />

<p>
  Failure is a value, not an exception, <code>match</code> handles both cases explicitly,
  the same honest way you handled <code>None</code>.
</p>

<Code
  code={`match safeDiv(10, 0) do
  Ok(x) -> x
  Error(msg) -> IO.printError("Oops: " + msg)
end`}
  repl
/>
